[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Classes and Objects in C#"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"C# is an object-oriented programming language. This means that it revolves around the concept of 'objects', which are instances of 'classes'. Understanding classes and objects is fundamental to writing effective C# code."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is a Class?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A class is a blueprint or a template for creating objects. It defines the properties (data) and methods (behavior) that objects of that class will have. Think of it as a cookie cutter; the class is the cutter, and the objects are the cookies."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"public class Dog
{
    // Properties
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public string Breed { get; set; }
    public int Age { get; set; }

    // Method
    public void Bark()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Woof!");
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, 'Dog' is a class. It has three properties: 'Name', 'Breed', and 'Age', and one method: 'Bark()'. The 'public' keyword indicates that these members are accessible from outside the class."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"What is an Object?"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"An object is an instance of a class. It's a concrete realization of the blueprint defined by the class. You can create multiple objects from a single class, each with its own unique set of property values."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"Dog myDog = new Dog();
myDog.Name = "Buddy";
myDog.Breed = "Golden Retriever";
myDog.Age = 3;

Console.WriteLine(myDog.Name); // Output: Buddy
myDog.Bark(); // Output: Woof!"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'myDog' is an object of the 'Dog' class. We create a new 'Dog' object using the 'new' keyword and then assign values to its properties. We can then call the 'Bark()' method on the 'myDog' object."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Concepts"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Encapsulation:** Bundling data (properties) and methods that operate on that data within a class. This helps to protect the data from outside access and modification."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Abstraction:** Hiding complex implementation details and exposing only the essential information to the user. This simplifies the use of objects and makes the code easier to understand."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Inheritance:** Creating new classes (derived classes) from existing classes (base classes). This allows you to reuse code and create a hierarchy of classes."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Polymorphism:** The ability of an object to take on many forms. This allows you to write code that can work with objects of different classes in a uniform way."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Constructors"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"A constructor is a special method that is automatically called when an object of a class is created. It's used to initialize the object's properties. If you don't define a constructor, C# provides a default constructor (without parameters)."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"public class Dog
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public string Breed { get; set; }
    public int Age { get; set; }

    // Constructor
    public Dog(string name, string breed, int age)
    {
        Name = name;
        Breed = breed;
        Age = age;
    }

    public void Bark()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Woof!");
    }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"Dog myDog = new Dog("Buddy", "Golden Retriever", 3);
Console.WriteLine(myDog.Name); // Output: Buddy"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Classes and objects are the building blocks of object-oriented programming in C#. By understanding these concepts, you can write more organized, reusable, and maintainable code. Mastering encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, and polymorphism will significantly improve your C# programming skills."}]
